WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:13.080
It's 9:00 AM.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:14.080
Good morning, everyone.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:18.510
I'm Alex Bui, the co-director of the BD2K
Center's Coordination Center.

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:23.529
And today we're continuing section 4 on data
modeling and inference methods.

00:00:23.529 --> 00:00:27.910
So one of the more intriguing and powerful
techniques that open up to us, to big data

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:31.850
science, is discovering testing of causal
mechanisms.

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:35.440
So rather than simply looking for statistical
correlations in the data, the objective is

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:40.670
to find more definitive, mechanistic relationships
from the data.

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:44.730
And as you can imagine, this is a tricky and
complex area of research.

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:51.430
I have the pleasure of introducing Dr. Joseph
Hogan, Professor of Biostatistics and of Public

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:52.900
Health at Brown University.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:57.600
He's also the Deputy Director of the Data
Science Initiative at Brown.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.230
His research interests include the development
of statistical methods for missing data, causal

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:09.471
inference, and sensitivity analysis, particularly,
in the areas around applications in HIV and

00:01:09.471 --> 00:01:10.710
behavioral sciences.

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:15.780
His recent work includes HIV studies in the
regions of Kenya and the sub-Saharan Africa.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:18.860
He'll be talking about causal inference methods
today.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:21.670
So please join me in welcoming Professor Hogan.

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:23.630
Joe, thank you for joining us today.

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:25.230
And the floor is yours.

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:29.170
Alex, thanks so much.

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:31.690
First I guess I should just checked to see
if you can hear me.

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:33.650
Yes we can.

00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:35.210
OK, great.

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:42.520
I'd like to thank Michelle Dunn, who is no
longer at NIH, but who is the one who invited

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:43.520
me to give this talk.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:46.210
And I'd like to thank the NIH for sponsoring
these lectures.

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:47.460
I've watched a couple of them.

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:49.170
They're really great.

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:55.600
And I expect they're going to be a terrific
resource for people as they navigate this

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:06.610
new terrain of data science.

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:14.129
So on this slide here I'm just going to outline
the goals of the causal inference presentation.

00:02:14.129 --> 00:02:17.980
The talk will be mostly conceptual.

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:20.730
I'll try to avoid technicalities.

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:28.690
And really the idea is to give a flavor for
causal thinking in observational studies,

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:34.360
and how to think about and adjust for confounding,
whether it's measured or unmeasured.

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:41.730
So first I'll talk about simple types of causal
questions using a very old clinical trial

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:47.870
that gave rise to a lot of causal inference
research in the '90s, the Clofibrate randomized

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:49.690
trial.

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:57.090
Next, formalize ways to think about causal
effects in terms of potential outcomes.

00:02:57.090 --> 00:03:01.709
And then, using another simple example, I'll
illustrate some statistical methods for inferring

00:03:01.709 --> 00:03:02.709
treatment effect.

00:03:02.709 --> 00:03:07.959
And I think this might be helpful for the
audience, because there are a lot of statistical

00:03:07.959 --> 00:03:08.959
techniques.

00:03:08.959 --> 00:03:13.550
And I think when they are illustrated on a
simple case, the essence of them becomes clear.

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:17.360
Although, their application in more complex
settings obviously demands more than what

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:18.360
can be covered in this lecture.

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:19.560
After that
application in more complex settings obviously

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:21.100
demands more than what can be covered in this
lecture.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:23.810
After that I'll discuss some more complicated
problems.

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:27.819
I'm going to focus on unmeasured confounding
and mediation.

00:03:27.819 --> 00:03:35.450
And then I'll just indicate some open questions
and big data problems in causal inference.

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:38.700
So I want to start with a disclaimer.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:43.590
First, causal inference is just too big for
a one hour webinar.

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:49.520
Here at Brown, we teach an entire semester
class on causal inference.

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:55.379
If you were to open most journals in statistics
or biostatistics, you'll see at least one,

00:03:55.379 --> 00:03:59.120
and probably more, articles on causal inference.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:03.570
And it's a field that's central to scientific
discovery.

00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:07.050
And it's growing all the time.

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:11.740
The examples that I'm going to use here reflect
my own uses of causal inference methods in

00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:12.800
applied problems.

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:15.209
So I'm not going to attempt to be comprehensive.

00:04:15.209 --> 00:04:22.120
And in fact, I'm going to leave out some very
important issues, or just briefly mention

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:23.120
them.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:27.580
Such as instrumental variables, which I'll
touch on a little bit when I talk about Mendelian

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:29.370
randomisation.

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:34.610
But graphical models is something that is
extremely important to causal inference and

00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:35.770
highly well developed.

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:38.349
But I won't have time to cover it here.

00:04:38.349 --> 00:04:41.389
And I just want to say that the omission of
these topics is really just a function of

00:04:41.389 --> 00:04:43.199
limited time.

00:04:43.199 --> 00:04:47.879
And it's not to reflect whether or not these
issues are important in the area of causal

00:04:47.879 --> 00:04:57.460
inference.

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:07.669
So causality and causal inference, these are
fundamental topics in science.

00:05:07.669 --> 00:05:15.029
And researchers in many fields have contributed,
not only to thinking about what makes a causal

00:05:15.029 --> 00:05:20.289
relationship causal, but also how to infer
causal relationships from observed data.

00:05:20.289 --> 00:05:21.539
And this is just an incomplete listing.

00:05:21.539 --> 00:05:25.479
And you can see there's a wide variety of
different disciplines where causal inference

00:05:25.479 --> 00:05:31.360
has played a significant role.

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:35.259
So I thought a little bit about what it means
to say causal inference.

00:05:35.259 --> 00:05:37.259
Or what do we mean by causal inference?

00:05:37.259 --> 00:05:41.889
And there are some different definitions out
there.

00:05:41.889 --> 00:05:45.099
If you open causal inference books, you might
see it described in different ways.

00:05:45.099 --> 00:05:51.379
But the way I think about it is it's a process
of using observed data to infer a causal relationship,

00:05:51.379 --> 00:05:55.789
based on a known or hypothesized mechanism
or model.

00:05:55.789 --> 00:05:57.690
And that could be a physical model.

00:05:57.690 --> 00:06:03.659
Or it could be a hypothesized mechanism that
we're using data to test out.

00:06:03.659 --> 00:06:12.240
There's a field of research, especially in
computer science and artificial intelligence,

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:17.399
concerned with discovering causal structure
from data on a purely empirical basis.

00:06:17.399 --> 00:06:23.849
But the focus here is going to be on inferring
causal relationships based on known or hypothesized

00:06:23.849 --> 00:06:26.060
mechanisms or models.

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:31.710
And there is a vast literature, as I mentioned,
on frameworks for causal inference, technical

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:36.300
approaches, methods for drawing inference,
and so forth.

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:42.580
So here I'm just going to show covers of some
books that have been published.

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:47.520
I think most of these have been published
in the last 10 years, if not all of them.

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:54.949
And it just really to indicate the vast sweep
of thinking on causal inference and also to

00:06:54.949 --> 00:06:57.889
indicate some of the leaders in this area.

00:06:57.889 --> 00:07:00.249
So these books are presented in no particular
order.

00:07:00.249 --> 00:07:06.919
But the first is a new book by Guido Imbens
and Donald Rubin called Causal Inference for

00:07:06.919 --> 00:07:10.350
Statistics, Social and Biomedical Sciences.

00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:15.839
In that book you'll find a detailed description
of potential outcomes, instrumental variables,

00:07:15.839 --> 00:07:18.280
and related techniques.

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:25.930
This book Causality by Judea Pearl is considered,
by now, a seminal text and lays out the so-called

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:32.199
do-calculus and graphical models for causal
reasoning and causal inference.

00:07:32.199 --> 00:07:39.419
These two books, the one on the left is a
little bit blurry, but it's the design of

00:07:39.419 --> 00:07:45.879
a cover for a book that's forthcoming by Miguel
Hernan and Jamie Robins.

00:07:45.879 --> 00:07:52.520
Next is a book on observational studies by
Paul Rosenbaum, who is at UPenn.

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:57.429
And then here are two new books that just
arrived on my desk.

00:07:57.429 --> 00:08:02.669
Explanation on Causal Inference is a book
that focuses on mediation and longitudinal

00:08:02.669 --> 00:08:06.300
causal processes by Tyler VanderWeele at Harvard.

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:11.249
And I would say Tyler is the leading authority
in this area of work.

00:08:11.249 --> 00:08:16.669
And a book that I found extremely useful for
teaching and that provides really a comprehensive

00:08:16.669 --> 00:08:22.849
coverage of a variety of different techniques
is Counterfactuals and Causal Inference by

00:08:22.849 --> 00:08:24.849
Morgan and Winship.

00:08:24.849 --> 00:08:29.169
And that book just came out with a second
edition that significantly expanded beyond

00:08:29.169 --> 00:08:30.169
the first.

00:08:30.169 --> 00:08:38.460
And I guess all these books would be good
starting points if one wanted to start to

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:41.430
dive into the world of causal inference.

00:08:41.430 --> 00:08:45.810
But my personal bias and the recommendation
I make to students is to start with the Morgan

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:54.060
and Winship book if you have some background
in statistics and some experience with regression

00:08:54.060 --> 00:09:00.380
and data analysis.

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:07.960
So the first part here concerns formalizing
a causal question.

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:17.020
And I'm just going to walk through some data
from a clinical trial of Clofibrate for reducing

00:09:17.020 --> 00:09:24.440
mortality in older patients with heart disease.

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:30.021
And by the way, I'll give citations to the
different papers that I cite, usually, with

00:09:30.021 --> 00:09:32.250
a short reference like I'm giving here.

00:09:32.250 --> 00:09:38.270
So hopefully that will give you enough to
look up the paper and learn some of the background.

00:09:38.270 --> 00:09:39.840
So if you look at this table.

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:46.470
This is a table from a clinical trial that
enrolled about 3,700 individuals.

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:54.130
And the way to read this is, if you look at
the top row, you'll see treatment group.

00:09:54.130 --> 00:09:56.070
You'll see Clofibrate and placebo.

00:09:56.070 --> 00:10:02.780
And in the Clofibrate arm, if you look to
the bottom row, you'll see that there were

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:06.380
1,065 randomized to Clofibrate.

00:10:06.380 --> 00:10:13.050
The mortality rate was about 18.2%, the five-year
mortality rate.

00:10:13.050 --> 00:10:16.960
And if you look at the placebo column and
you go to the bottom row, you'll see there

00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:21.270
were 2,695 randomized to the placebo.

00:10:21.270 --> 00:10:24.510
And the mortality rate was about 19%.

00:10:24.510 --> 00:10:30.400
Now what this table also does is it subdivides
these groups into those who were considered

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:35.140
adherent with the treatment and not adherent.

00:10:35.140 --> 00:10:41.320
Adherence is defined as taking the medicine
as prescribed more than 80% of the time.

00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:46.250
And so just to see how to read this you'll
see that, for example, the row that says less

00:10:46.250 --> 00:10:53.290
than 80% in the Clofibrate arm, 357 patients
were not complying.

00:10:53.290 --> 00:10:57.780
And the mortality rate in that group was 24%.

00:10:57.780 --> 00:11:03.400
In the placebo arm, which
had a simulated version of the drug so that

00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:08.720
it looked like you were taking Clofibrate,
882 were not compliant.

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:11.310
The mortality rate there was 28%.

00:11:11.310 --> 00:11:17.310
And then in the compliant or adherent groups,
it was 15% mortality rate in each.

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:20.280
So that's how to decompose this table.

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:27.840
But even in a simple table like this, we can
frame at least three different causal questions.

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:32.260
And so this really goes to the first part
of the talk, which is, how do we think about

00:11:32.260 --> 00:11:33.340
causal effects?

00:11:33.340 --> 00:11:37.980
A generic way to ask the question is, well,
what is the effect of Clofibrate?

00:11:37.980 --> 00:11:43.370
But the starting point for causal evidence
demands that the question be framed in more

00:11:43.370 --> 00:11:45.340
specific terms than that.

00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:47.850
So here are three examples.

00:11:47.850 --> 00:11:55.860
The first example is like a policy question,
or a question about public health practice

00:11:55.860 --> 00:12:00.500
or general treatment of heart disease.

00:12:00.500 --> 00:12:05.320
And so the premise is that doctors are going
to prescribe Clofibrate.

00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:07.250
Will it reduce mortality?

00:12:07.250 --> 00:12:12.310
And there, we're asking what is the causal
effect of being randomized to Clofibrate.

00:12:12.310 --> 00:12:15.270
That's how we can answer that question with
these data.

00:12:15.270 --> 00:12:18.661
The second question is different though, if
you think about it.

00:12:18.661 --> 00:12:20.150
It's more at the individual level.

00:12:20.150 --> 00:12:23.780
And sometimes it is a question that we might
have if we go to the doctor.

00:12:23.780 --> 00:12:25.760
My doctor gave me Clofibrate.

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:29.380
If I take it, will it reduce my chances of
early mortality?

00:12:29.380 --> 00:12:33.780
And so here we're talking about the causal
effect of actually taking Clofibrate, rather

00:12:33.780 --> 00:12:35.640
than just being randomized.

00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:42.130
We might also know that there's a group of
patients who elected to take the drug.

00:12:42.130 --> 00:12:48.000
And we might ask the question, if they derived
benefit from taking a drug, relative to not

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:49.430
taking it?

00:12:49.430 --> 00:12:54.560
So we could ask, what is the causal effect
of taking Clofibrate, but just among the subgroup

00:12:54.560 --> 00:13:00.530
of individuals who took the drug.

00:13:00.530 --> 00:13:02.290
So we'll take these one at a time.

00:13:02.290 --> 00:13:08.560
And we'll look at how the data can be used
to either answer these questions or maybe

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:12.750
to gain some intuition, but not really formally
answer the questions.

00:13:12.750 --> 00:13:18.570
So the first is the question about overall
prescribing patterns.

00:13:18.570 --> 00:13:27.200
So we can ask a question about, if doctors
prescribe Clofibrate, will it reduce mortality?

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:34.940
And this is the kind of question that goes
to general practice patterns.

00:13:34.940 --> 00:13:41.780
So should Clofibrate be prescribed for these
sorts of patients as a group.

00:13:41.780 --> 00:13:48.460
So to pin down the answer, we can frame it
as the causal effect of being randomized to

00:13:48.460 --> 00:13:49.990
Clofibrate.

00:13:49.990 --> 00:13:53.680
We have the answer to that question right
here in this trial.

00:13:53.680 --> 00:13:57.110
Because we have one group of patients who
was prescribed Clofibrate, by virtue of being

00:13:57.110 --> 00:14:04.360
randomized to that arm, and the second group
that was not prescribed Clofibrate.

00:14:04.360 --> 00:14:09.190
And if we look at the difference, we see the
mortality rates are very similar.

00:14:09.190 --> 00:14:17.600
There's not really much of a reduction in
mortality, maybe about a 1.2% absolute reduction.

00:14:17.600 --> 00:14:22.420
The next question has to do with the individual.

00:14:22.420 --> 00:14:25.500
So my doctor gave me Clofibrate.

00:14:25.500 --> 00:14:29.090
If I take it, will it reduce my chances of
early mortality?

00:14:29.090 --> 00:14:32.010
Now this question is a little more complicated
to answer.

00:14:32.010 --> 00:14:35.400
And it's not entirely clear how
we would answer it from the data that are

00:14:35.400 --> 00:14:37.480
presented.

00:14:37.480 --> 00:14:44.360
So our intuition might be to focus on just
the patients that were randomized to the Clofibrate

00:14:44.360 --> 00:14:45.390
arm.

00:14:45.390 --> 00:14:50.660
And what we see is that among those who took
the drug, the mortality rate was 15%.

00:14:50.660 --> 00:14:55.950
But there was a substantial number of individuals
who didn't take the drug.

00:14:55.950 --> 00:14:59.390
And the mortality rate there was 24%.

00:14:59.390 --> 00:15:04.630
So just by virtue of numbers, we might think
we can get a comparison.

00:15:04.630 --> 00:15:09.410
But there's nothing that tells us that these
groups are similar on other characteristics

00:15:09.410 --> 00:15:11.180
that might be related to mortality.

00:15:11.180 --> 00:15:17.270
And in fact, if we look at the next column
in the placebo arm, we'll see that those who

00:15:17.270 --> 00:15:23.790
complied with placebo also had a lower mortality
rate than those who didn't comply.

00:15:23.790 --> 00:15:27.960
So we're not going to go and try to untangle
this right here.

00:15:27.960 --> 00:15:34.060
But this is just an illustration of the fact
that we can't simply use a two group comparison,

00:15:34.060 --> 00:15:38.760
even within this randomized trial, to answer
the question of whether taking Clofibrate

00:15:38.760 --> 00:15:47.120
is going to have a causal effect on mortality.

00:15:47.120 --> 00:15:52.990
Now another question is some people took Clofibrate
and some didn't-- among those who took it,

00:15:52.990 --> 00:15:54.910
did it reduce early mortality?

00:15:54.910 --> 00:16:01.720
Well here, we see there's only one group of
individuals that took Clofibrate.

00:16:01.720 --> 00:16:07.460
And the other difficulty is that it would
be hard, it's not obvious at least, to find

00:16:07.460 --> 00:16:13.340
the control group that would correspond to
this group that's circled in red in order

00:16:13.340 --> 00:16:20.190
for us to make this causal comparison.

00:16:20.190 --> 00:16:25.350
So the take-aways from this are that first,
we need to be precise about our target of

00:16:25.350 --> 00:16:26.350
inference.

00:16:26.350 --> 00:16:30.550
And just by asking the questions the way we
did, we can be precise about what we're interested

00:16:30.550 --> 00:16:32.790
in.

00:16:32.790 --> 00:16:37.410
But looking at the observed data may not lead
to intuitive or correct answers.

00:16:37.410 --> 00:16:41.800
And in particular, we can't just compare,
for example, those who complied with the drug

00:16:41.800 --> 00:16:45.730
versus those who didn't to try to address
a causal question.

00:16:45.730 --> 00:16:52.520
So that gives rise to the need for a causal
framework for representing causal effects,

00:16:52.520 --> 00:17:00.770
and then mapping those representations to
observed data to try to draw inference about

00:17:00.770 --> 00:17:05.820
causal effects.

00:17:05.820 --> 00:17:10.360
So the framework I'm going to describe here
is a potential outcomes framework.

00:17:10.360 --> 00:17:13.930
It's a formal way to represent causal effects.

00:17:13.930 --> 00:17:21.629
It's due to Don Rubin, who wrote about potential
outcomes in a 1974 paper in the educational

00:17:21.629 --> 00:17:24.199
psychology literature.

00:17:24.199 --> 00:17:32.759
The potential outcomes, one reason I like
to use them is that they expose what's known

00:17:32.759 --> 00:17:38.850
as the fundamental problem of causal inference,
which I'll talk about in a minute.

00:17:38.850 --> 00:17:43.570
Potential outcomes can also help us understand
how to adjust for confounding.

00:17:43.570 --> 00:17:50.749
And we can frame confounding as a selection
problem which naturally leads to some of the

00:17:50.749 --> 00:17:53.399
more common methods for confounder adjustment.

00:17:53.399 --> 00:18:00.970
So after we lay out the potential outcomes
framework, I'll walk through some statistical

00:18:00.970 --> 00:18:05.789
methods for confounder adjustment and a simple
example, really for the purpose of contrasting

00:18:05.789 --> 00:18:13.240
how they work.

00:18:13.240 --> 00:18:19.470
So the potential outcomes framework for defining
causal effects can be described pretty easily

00:18:19.470 --> 00:18:22.240
in the case of binary treatment.

00:18:22.240 --> 00:18:29.770
For binary treatment, we have the label a
is equal to 1 if a person is exposed and 0

00:18:29.770 --> 00:18:30.770
if not.

00:18:30.770 --> 00:18:31.770
And this is not a random
variable.

00:18:31.770 --> 00:18:33.860
It's just a label.

00:18:33.860 --> 00:18:39.480
And so the potential outcomes framework assumes
that each person has two potential outcomes

00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:55.990
in this setting, Y1 if the person has been
exposed and Y0 if not.

00:18:55.990 --> 00:19:03.629
So in this simple setting, we can define causal
effects in terms of potential outcomes.

00:19:03.629 --> 00:19:10.240
At the individual level, we can define contrasts
of potential outcomes to be our causal effect,

00:19:10.240 --> 00:19:12.970
simply Y1 minus Y0.

00:19:12.970 --> 00:19:17.669
At the population level, we can take an average
over a population of interest.

00:19:17.669 --> 00:19:23.120
So we take the average difference between
Y1 and Y0.

00:19:23.120 --> 00:19:26.080
And we can also define causal effects within
subgroups.

00:19:26.080 --> 00:19:29.830
So for example, we could look at the causal
effect within women.

00:19:29.830 --> 00:19:34.890
We could look at the causal effect among people
who took a treatment, for example.

00:19:34.890 --> 00:19:38.950
Differences in potential outcomes are not
the only way potential causal effects can

00:19:38.950 --> 00:19:40.850
be defined.

00:19:40.850 --> 00:19:50.940
They can also be defined in terms of ratios
or other functions of Y1 and Y0.

00:19:50.940 --> 00:19:56.769
So the fundamental problem of causal inference
is that the potential outcomes can never be

00:19:56.769 --> 00:19:58.790
fully observed.

00:19:58.790 --> 00:20:03.899
And this is actually what leads to the term
counter-factual.

00:20:03.899 --> 00:20:09.750
With the binary treatment, in this case, only
one of the potential outcomes is observed.

00:20:09.750 --> 00:20:15.919
And the one that's not observed is counter-factual.

00:20:15.919 --> 00:20:19.419
So let's just look at a simple example.

00:20:19.419 --> 00:20:21.239
I do a lot of work on HIV.

00:20:21.239 --> 00:20:27.070
And so this hypothetical example will just
look at setting up potential outcomes for

00:20:27.070 --> 00:20:31.919
examining the effect of HIV treatment on survival.

00:20:31.919 --> 00:20:37.309
So we're going to define little a to be the
treatment label, 1 or 0.

00:20:37.309 --> 00:20:45.330
And this would be a scenario that upon presentation
with HIV, an individual either receives anti-retroviral

00:20:45.330 --> 00:20:49.500
therapy, or ART, or does not.

00:20:49.500 --> 00:20:57.080
And so for this individual, the potential
outcomes are Y0 and Y1.

00:20:57.080 --> 00:21:03.460
Where Y0 is a survival time under the scenario
that the person does not receive treatment.

00:21:03.460 --> 00:21:09.989
And Y1 is the survival time under the scenario
that the person does receive treatment.

00:21:09.989 --> 00:21:21.179
And the causal effect of receiving ART is
the contrast of these two.

00:21:21.179 --> 00:21:26.289
Now the way potential outcomes relate to observed
data is as follows.

00:21:26.289 --> 00:21:34.509
If you remember, the treatment label is little
a, either 1 or 0.

00:21:34.509 --> 00:21:38.929
But the actual treatment received, the random
variable, we're going to denote with capital

00:21:38.929 --> 00:21:48.139
A. So capital A is 1 if the individual actually
received the treatment and 0 if not.

00:21:48.139 --> 00:21:53.970
So the observed outcome is either going to
be Y1 or Y0 depending on whether treatment

00:21:53.970 --> 00:21:56.989
was received or not.

00:21:56.989 --> 00:22:05.580
And I'll walk you through a simple example
to show that the expression on the left, which

00:22:05.580 --> 00:22:10.369
is the average outcome for people who have
received the treatment minus the average outcome

00:22:10.369 --> 00:22:17.360
for people who did not receive the treatment,
is not the same as the contrast in the average

00:22:17.360 --> 00:22:19.899
of the potential outcomes.

00:22:19.899 --> 00:22:33.159
So this expression at the bottom is a compact
way of saying that causation is not association.

00:22:33.159 --> 00:22:35.320
So here's a hypothetical example.

00:22:35.320 --> 00:22:41.529
And for those who have read introductory books
on causal inference, this might look familiar.

00:22:41.529 --> 00:22:50.970
So what I've done is I've written down hypothetical
data, data that we can't see, for seven individuals.

00:22:50.970 --> 00:22:56.529
And I just want to write down the underlying
potential outcomes for these seven individuals.

00:22:56.529 --> 00:23:01.600
So what you'll see is for person 1, we have
Y1 and Y0.

00:23:01.600 --> 00:23:08.419
Y0 is 300, which means that if person 1 had
not received treatment, they would

00:23:08.419 --> 00:23:10.710
survive for 300 days.

00:23:10.710 --> 00:23:15.009
And if they did receive treatment, they would
survive for 500 days.

00:23:15.009 --> 00:23:19.960
So the causal effect of receiving treatment
for person 1 is 200.

00:23:19.960 --> 00:23:28.399
Likewise, if we go down the list for person
2, if they receive treatment, they will survive

00:23:28.399 --> 00:23:29.899
for 900 days.

00:23:29.899 --> 00:23:33.429
If they don't receive treatment, they would
only survive for 200 days.

00:23:33.429 --> 00:23:39.410
So the causal effect is to increase their
survival by 700 days.

00:23:39.410 --> 00:23:49.860
If we tally the averages for Y0 and Y1, we
see that average survival on treatment is

00:23:49.860 --> 00:23:51.420
843.

00:23:51.420 --> 00:23:55.630
That's the average survival under the scenario
that everybody receives treatment.

00:23:55.630 --> 00:24:02.210
And the average survival under the scenario
that nobody receives treatment is 529.

00:24:02.210 --> 00:24:09.789
So the causal effect of treating everyone
versus treating no one is 314.

00:24:09.789 --> 00:24:20.259
Now, this next table shows an allocation of
treatment as individuals present with HIV.

00:24:20.259 --> 00:24:25.360
So what this table shows is that the first
four individuals will receive treatment.

00:24:25.360 --> 00:24:34.519
And the next three will not receive treatment.

00:24:34.519 --> 00:24:39.409
So for those who receive treatment, the first
four individuals, the outcome we get to see

00:24:39.409 --> 00:24:42.360
is Y1.

00:24:42.360 --> 00:24:47.390
And for the next three individuals who did
not receive treatment, the outcome we get

00:24:47.390 --> 00:24:50.799
to see is Y0.

00:24:50.799 --> 00:25:06.350
So the observed data are in the last column,
the yvariable.

00:25:06.350 --> 00:25:12.149
If we look at the association between treatment
and outcome, we can start by taking the average

00:25:12.149 --> 00:25:14.590
of Y for those who received the treatment.

00:25:14.590 --> 00:25:16.320
And that is 600.

00:25:16.320 --> 00:25:20.489
And we could take the average of Y for those
who did not receive treatment, and we see

00:25:20.489 --> 00:25:27.289
that's 867.

00:25:27.289 --> 00:25:33.789
So the difference in these two averages is
what I'm calling the predictive effect.

00:25:33.789 --> 00:25:42.480
What I mean by that is that we could use the
indicator of whether a person received treatment

00:25:42.480 --> 00:25:45.549
to predict their survival.

00:25:45.549 --> 00:25:51.140
So in other words, if we look at the predictive
effect as the difference in Y between those

00:25:51.140 --> 00:25:55.059
who received and did not receive treatment,
we see that those who actually did receive

00:25:55.059 --> 00:26:01.610
treatment survived for 267 days less on average.

00:26:01.610 --> 00:26:07.519
So receiving treatment actually has a negative
predictive effect on survival.

00:26:07.519 --> 00:26:11.100
But that's not the same as the causal effect.

00:26:11.100 --> 00:26:15.570
The causal effect is the difference in the
potential outcomes, which, as we saw previously,

00:26:15.570 --> 00:26:26.889
is to increase survival by 314 days.

00:26:26.889 --> 00:26:33.320
So this discrepancy raises the question of,
how can we use statistical methods and observational

00:26:33.320 --> 00:26:37.649
data to handle this phenomenon?

00:26:37.649 --> 00:26:44.169
And if I just go back one slide, the discrepancy
here between the predictive effect and the

00:26:44.169 --> 00:26:54.739
causal effect could potentially be explained
by what are called confounding variables.

00:26:54.739 --> 00:27:00.080
So now we'll expand the inference problem
to include other characteristics that may

00:27:00.080 --> 00:27:03.369
be observed on the individual.

00:27:03.369 --> 00:27:10.919
So the observed data for an individual we'll
say are given by A, Y, and X.

00:27:10.919 --> 00:27:12.679
And these are random variables.

00:27:12.679 --> 00:27:18.460
And we say that A is 1 if the treatment is
received and 0 if not.

00:27:18.460 --> 00:27:23.570
We see that Y is going to be either Y1 or
Y0, depending on whether treatment is received.

00:27:23.570 --> 00:27:26.549
And X is a set of measured covariates.

00:27:26.549 --> 00:27:30.830
And so the question is, how do we use a sample
of observed data of this form to draw inference

00:27:30.830 --> 00:27:38.499
about an average treatment effect?

00:27:38.499 --> 00:27:42.539
So one data example I can use to illustrate
this is the HIV epidemiology research study,

00:27:42.539 --> 00:27:43.539
or the
treatment effect?

00:27:43.539 --> 00:27:44.539
So one data example I can use to illustrate
this is the HIV epidemiology research study,

00:27:44.539 --> 00:27:47.490
or the HER study.

00:27:47.490 --> 00:27:53.590
It's a cohort study of HIV in women that took
place early in the epidemic, in the 1990s.

00:27:53.590 --> 00:28:00.749
And what this table shows is a simple two-group
comparison of those who received therapy,

00:28:00.749 --> 00:28:05.440
which is the A equals 1 column, and those
who didn't, which is A equals 0 column.

00:28:05.440 --> 00:28:14.519
And the outcome we're interested in looking
at is CD4 at 6 months, following a baseline

00:28:14.519 --> 00:28:16.669
entry time.

00:28:16.669 --> 00:28:22.749
So the covariates that we can look at are
baseline, the log viral load.

00:28:22.749 --> 00:28:25.080
And higher is worse.

00:28:25.080 --> 00:28:31.890
We can look at baseline symptoms, and higher
is worse in that case, and baseline CD4, where

00:28:31.890 --> 00:28:34.019
higher is better.

00:28:34.019 --> 00:28:39.769
So what we see in the A equals 1 column is
that the CD4 at six months was slightly higher

00:28:39.769 --> 00:28:41.889
than for A equals 0.

00:28:41.889 --> 00:28:49.870
But the covariates indicate those who were
less healthy are more likely to receive the

00:28:49.870 --> 00:28:50.870
treatment.

00:28:50.870 --> 00:29:01.869
And that's similar to the hypothetical data
we saw on the previous slides.

00:29:01.869 --> 00:29:06.769
So just to show how the observed data relate
to the potential outcomes and how the observed

00:29:06.769 --> 00:29:11.220
data are actually an incomplete representation
of the information we need to draw causal

00:29:11.220 --> 00:29:12.220
effects.

00:29:12.220 --> 00:29:15.929
Here are a couple of excerpts from the observed
data.

00:29:15.929 --> 00:29:17.289
We can look at the control group.

00:29:17.289 --> 00:29:19.299
And the first column is treatment.

00:29:19.299 --> 00:29:21.630
We see that all of them have A equals 0.

00:29:21.630 --> 00:29:26.539
Then X1, X2, X3 are the covariates.

00:29:26.539 --> 00:29:28.470
Y is the observed outcome.

00:29:28.470 --> 00:29:35.389
But the important comps to look at are Y0
and Y1.

00:29:35.389 --> 00:29:39.239
So for the control group, we only get to observe
Y0.

00:29:39.239 --> 00:29:43.649
And if we look at the treatment group, we
only get to observe Y1.

00:29:43.649 --> 00:29:52.869
So in order to use observed confounders to
make adjustments, we need to define a concept

00:29:52.869 --> 00:29:55.070
known as ignorable treatment assignment.

00:29:55.070 --> 00:30:01.690
A treatment assignment is ignorable if we
have some subset of the confounders such that

00:30:01.690 --> 00:30:08.340
we can say treatment is randomly allocated
within distinct levels of the confounders.

00:30:08.340 --> 00:30:15.700
So we have to be able to find subsets of individuals
that are the same on their X values but different

00:30:15.700 --> 00:30:18.289
on their treatment values.

00:30:18.289 --> 00:30:23.990
And we have to make the assumption that the
difference in the treatment values is unrelated

00:30:23.990 --> 00:30:26.210
to the potential outcomes.

00:30:26.210 --> 00:30:40.260
If this condition doesn't hold, then we have
a problem known as unmeasured confounding.

00:30:40.260 --> 00:30:45.990
An important summary of the confounding information
and causal inference is the propensity score.

00:30:45.990 --> 00:30:52.359
The propensity score is a summary measure
that represents the probability of receiving

00:30:52.359 --> 00:30:54.559
treatment conditional on covariates.

00:30:54.559 --> 00:31:07.320
And a key result, in brief, is that if treatment
is ignorable, conditional on the covariates

00:31:07.320 --> 00:31:14.929
X, and if the propensity score is bounded
between 0 and 1, which means that every distinct

00:31:14.929 --> 00:31:21.340
X value has some probability of receiving
treatment, then we can say treatment is also

00:31:21.340 --> 00:31:23.669
ignorable, conditional on the propensity score.

00:31:23.669 --> 00:31:31.159
A way to say this in plain English is that
we can just condition on the propensity score

00:31:31.159 --> 00:31:38.210
to do causal inference, rather than conditioning
on all the covariates at once.

00:31:38.210 --> 00:31:45.179
Now there's several important consequences
to this finding.

00:31:45.179 --> 00:31:49.729
Propensity scores can be used for confounder
adjustment in a number of ways.

00:31:49.729 --> 00:31:57.259
One very popular way of using propensity scores
is to do inverse probability weighting.

00:31:57.259 --> 00:32:05.429
And in this case, each observation is weighted
by the inverse probability of the treatment

00:32:05.429 --> 00:32:06.989
that person
received.

00:32:06.989 --> 00:32:12.210
So in a sense, it's correcting a non-random
treatment allocation problem.

00:32:12.210 --> 00:32:18.549
And when you have a weighted sample, you can
then go ahead and apply standard techniques

00:32:18.549 --> 00:32:22.509
to infer the treatment effect.

00:32:22.509 --> 00:32:27.549
So more details about this are in the book
I mentioned before by Miguel Hernan and Jamie

00:32:27.549 --> 00:32:28.549
Robins.

00:32:28.549 --> 00:32:31.889
And I've given the link there.

00:32:31.889 --> 00:32:37.500
Matching is another technique that is important
and useful for drawing causal effects based

00:32:37.500 --> 00:32:38.760
on propensity scores.

00:32:38.760 --> 00:32:44.679
I'm going to talk a little bit about matching
so that a couple of subtle issues will become

00:32:44.679 --> 00:32:46.970
clearer.

00:32:46.970 --> 00:32:54.099
I found that in my own collaborations, anecdotally,
the use propensity score for matching is something

00:32:54.099 --> 00:33:00.929
that generates a fair bit of confusion.

00:33:00.929 --> 00:33:05.590
So matching can be used to create matched
pairs or sets having similar propensity scores.

00:33:05.590 --> 00:33:12.759
But the confusion arises, in some cases, because
in matching what we can do is we can create

00:33:12.759 --> 00:33:17.129
balanced groups, in the sense that the confounder
distributions are balanced between the treat

00:33:17.129 --> 00:33:18.870
and control groups.

00:33:18.870 --> 00:33:25.649
But matching up propensity scores is not the
same as matching on individual covariate values.

00:33:25.649 --> 00:33:35.039
And in particular, it's possible to have individuals
that have almost identical propensity scores

00:33:35.039 --> 00:33:38.279
but very different covariate values.

00:33:38.279 --> 00:33:48.950
So matching is good for group level inference,
but not necessarily individual level inference.

00:33:48.950 --> 00:33:56.120
So in matching, the general idea is to refine
matches by minimizing the distance between

00:33:56.120 --> 00:34:03.830
covariates, between the treated and control
groups, or possibly the propensity scores.

00:34:03.830 --> 00:34:07.769
So there are lots of different metrics we
can use for matching.

00:34:07.769 --> 00:34:12.050
Exact matching means that we'll declare a
match only if the covariate values are exactly

00:34:12.050 --> 00:34:13.050
the same.

00:34:13.050 --> 00:34:16.700
And in practice, this tends to be a little
bit difficult.

00:34:16.700 --> 00:34:22.490
There are also Euclidean distance measures,
or Mahalanobis distance measures, looking

00:34:22.490 --> 00:34:27.300
at the statistical distance between the covariates.

00:34:27.300 --> 00:34:36.610
And we can also use the propensity score as
a distance measure.

00:34:36.610 --> 00:34:42.890
So one source of confusion with matching has
to do with what quantity is being estimated.

00:34:42.890 --> 00:34:47.600
There are two basic types of matching in the
two-sample problem, treatment group matching

00:34:47.600 --> 00:34:50.230
and full matching.

00:34:50.230 --> 00:34:54.020
So in treatment group matching, what we do
is, for each person in the treatment group,

00:34:54.020 --> 00:34:55.960
we find matches from the control group.

00:34:55.960 --> 00:35:00.280
And this actually estimates the average treatment
effect among the treated.

00:35:00.280 --> 00:35:04.300
The reason being that we're sampling, initially,
from the treatment group, and then looking

00:35:04.300 --> 00:35:09.120
into the control group to find individual
matches.

00:35:09.120 --> 00:35:13.510
In full matching, we don't first sample on
the treatment group.

00:35:13.510 --> 00:35:20.390
What we do is we just look across all possible
pairs and find pairs or groups that are close

00:35:20.390 --> 00:35:22.460
on the matching criterion.

00:35:22.460 --> 00:35:26.810
And when we match using full matching, we'll
be estimating the average treatment effect.

00:35:26.810 --> 00:35:29.040
Because we're not starting with a sample of
treated individuals.

00:35:29.040 --> 00:35:37.630
Then there is a whole raft of algorithms that
are designed to create optimal matches.

00:35:37.630 --> 00:35:49.260
Most of them are versions of minimizing the
sum of the distance measure across the sample.

00:35:49.260 --> 00:35:52.570
So I've mentioned this already, but it's just
important to keep in mind the question of

00:35:52.570 --> 00:35:54.930
matching versus balance.

00:35:54.930 --> 00:36:00.130
Matching on propensity scores does not imply
matching on covariates.

00:36:00.130 --> 00:36:04.410
And matching propensity scores is not
designed to create pairs that can be looked

00:36:04.410 --> 00:36:05.690
at individually.

00:36:05.690 --> 00:36:10.720
They're really designed to create a balanced
distribution in two different groups that

00:36:10.720 --> 00:36:15.490
are being compared.

00:36:15.490 --> 00:36:26.000
So next I'll just show a brief illustration
using the HERS data and just using two of

00:36:26.000 --> 00:36:34.830
the covariates, CD4 cell count and symptom
score at enrollment 

00:36:34.830 --> 00:36:41.470
So these are just excerpts from an analysis,
but to give you an idea that these methods

00:36:41.470 --> 00:36:45.820
are relatively easy to apply.

00:36:45.820 --> 00:36:48.510
So first we can fit a propensity score model.

00:36:48.510 --> 00:36:52.100
And there's only two covariates here, so we're
just going to use the main effects.

00:36:52.100 --> 00:36:56.130
Although, I'm going to say a couple of things
about situations, big data situations, where

00:36:56.130 --> 00:37:04.670
we have many covariates, possibly even thousands
of covariates.

00:37:04.670 --> 00:37:09.710
After fitting our propensity score model,
we use the estimated propensity scores to

00:37:09.710 --> 00:37:11.400
find matched pairs.

00:37:11.400 --> 00:37:17.400
And then a very important step is to look
at the covariate distributions to ensure that

00:37:17.400 --> 00:37:19.450
there's some balance.

00:37:19.450 --> 00:37:23.100
And once you're confident that you have balanced
covariates, actually the nice thing about

00:37:23.100 --> 00:37:28.720
matching is you can simply perform analyses
using standard methods, like two-sample comparisons

00:37:28.720 --> 00:37:31.420
or regression estimators.

00:37:31.420 --> 00:37:39.500
In some matching setups, you have to use weighted
regression because of the unequal representation

00:37:39.500 --> 00:37:44.090
of groups in the match sets.

00:37:44.090 --> 00:37:49.860
So one consequence of some matching algorithms,
depending on how you set it up, is that you

00:37:49.860 --> 00:37:52.530
might be discarding a lot of your data.

00:37:52.530 --> 00:37:59.800
So here we started with 246 in the control
group and 111 in the treated group.

00:37:59.800 --> 00:38:03.590
This analysis is going to be estimating the
average treatment effect among the treated.

00:38:03.590 --> 00:38:09.200
So we took the 111 treated individuals and
matched them to 111 controls, which means

00:38:09.200 --> 00:38:16.510
that 135 were left out of the analysis.

00:38:16.510 --> 00:38:21.480
So there were a lot of empirical ways of looking
at whether balance has been achieved.

00:38:21.480 --> 00:38:26.290
In this case, if you look at the first column,
we see propensity scores for the treated and

00:38:26.290 --> 00:38:27.290
control groups.

00:38:27.290 --> 00:38:30.310
And those histograms don't look very similar.

00:38:30.310 --> 00:38:34.610
But in the second column, if we look at the
matched individuals, the propensity score

00:38:34.610 --> 00:38:38.500
histograms are almost identical.

00:38:38.500 --> 00:38:44.080
And I think I'll skip this plot, because I'm
keeping an eye on the time.

00:38:44.080 --> 00:38:50.950
And I want to be able to mention a couple
of topics that are coming up.

00:38:50.950 --> 00:38:59.860
So to do analysis after the matching, we can
do a simple two-sample test.

00:38:59.860 --> 00:39:04.570
This right here is the result of a t-test
or a regression with just a binary covariate.

00:39:04.570 --> 00:39:07.900
And we see that the causal effect is 27.

00:39:07.900 --> 00:39:16.250
But most papers on matching recommend also
controlling for the covariates that have been

00:39:16.250 --> 00:39:21.720
matched on as a way of increasing efficiency
and cleaning up any possible discrepancies.

00:39:21.720 --> 00:39:27.070
So here we see that the estimate is about
the same.

00:39:27.070 --> 00:39:32.530
But if we just flip back, the standard error
of the treatment effect was 22, without adjusting

00:39:32.530 --> 00:39:34.270
for covariates.

00:39:34.270 --> 00:39:36.890
And it is 12, after we do adjust for covariates.

00:39:36.890 --> 00:39:42.050
So there can be considerable efficiency gains
by adjusting for the covariates that were

00:39:42.050 --> 00:39:46.480
matched on.

00:39:46.480 --> 00:39:51.120
So another common technique for causal inference
is extrapolation.

00:39:51.120 --> 00:39:55.020
And in some more complicated settings where
the extrapolation takes place is known as

00:39:55.020 --> 00:39:57.160
the G-computation algorithm.

00:39:57.160 --> 00:40:01.020
And again, it's described in the
book by Hernan and Robins.

00:40:01.020 --> 00:40:07.580
And again, the idea is if we view causal inference
through the lens of potential outcomes, the

00:40:07.580 --> 00:40:10.280
approach here becomes pretty transparent.

00:40:10.280 --> 00:40:16.700
We want to impute the missing Y1 and Y0.

00:40:16.700 --> 00:40:21.990
So that can be done in this very simple case
using three steps.

00:40:21.990 --> 00:40:26.070
We fit a regression model for Y given X in
the treatment group.

00:40:26.070 --> 00:40:30.780
And that gives us a sense of prediction model
for Y1.

00:40:30.780 --> 00:40:36.400
And we fit a regression of Y given X in the
control arm, and that gives us, essentially,

00:40:36.400 --> 00:40:41.320
an imputation or prediction model for Y0.

00:40:41.320 --> 00:40:48.560
We then apply these prediction models to the
entire sample to get a difference that represents

00:40:48.560 --> 00:40:53.650
the average treatment effect.

00:40:53.650 --> 00:40:58.810
So just to remind you, these are excerpts
from the observed data.

00:40:58.810 --> 00:41:03.850
What you'll notice is that the Y1 column is
missing for the control group.

00:41:03.850 --> 00:41:08.960
And the Y0 column is missing for the treatment
group.

00:41:08.960 --> 00:41:15.481
But after the fitting of regression models
and imputing the missing outcomes, what we

00:41:15.481 --> 00:41:20.310
get is for the control group, we have both
Y0 and Y1.

00:41:20.310 --> 00:41:24.090
And despite this being the control group,
we can use the predictions for Y0.

00:41:24.090 --> 00:41:30.500
And in the treatment group, we have Y0 and
Y1.

00:41:30.500 --> 00:41:35.350
And so the causal inference is really a matter
of just taking the average of the differences,

00:41:35.350 --> 00:41:42.460
which appear in the last column.

00:41:42.460 --> 00:41:47.860
And so causal inference using a computation
algorithm can be compared to the unadjusted

00:41:47.860 --> 00:41:49.160
estimate here.

00:41:49.160 --> 00:41:54.800
And what we see is that the unadjusted estimate
showed about a 10% difference in CD4 counts.

00:41:54.800 --> 00:42:05.010
Whereas, applying the computation algorithm
boosted that to about 30.

00:42:05.010 --> 00:42:09.210
So just a couple of notes about how this may
scale up to big data.

00:42:09.210 --> 00:42:15.530
There is a lot of people asking a question,
does big data solve of the problem of causal

00:42:15.530 --> 00:42:16.530
inference?

00:42:16.530 --> 00:42:21.250
In the sense that, well, we don't have tens
or hundreds or thousands of confounders, or

00:42:21.250 --> 00:42:24.070
we may have massive data sets.

00:42:24.070 --> 00:42:30.640
So an important component of what I just talked
about, in whether using propensity scores

00:42:30.640 --> 00:42:35.260
or regression models, is that these models
have to be correctly specified.

00:42:35.260 --> 00:42:40.200
And with many confounders, it's frequently
hard to know what the correct specification

00:42:40.200 --> 00:42:41.200
is.

00:42:41.200 --> 00:42:44.970
So this has given rise to a large body of
work where machine learning techniques are

00:42:44.970 --> 00:42:49.320
used to fit these submodels.

00:42:49.320 --> 00:42:55.220
And we can also look at techniques which are
robust in and misspecification of either the

00:42:55.220 --> 00:42:58.350
treatment model or the outcome model.

00:42:58.350 --> 00:43:03.470
And a good starting point for learning about
some of these models is in a book by Mark

00:43:03.470 --> 00:43:07.620
van der Laan and Sherri Rose known as Targeted
Learning.

00:43:07.620 --> 00:43:14.060
There's a really comprehensive coverage of
this approach in that book.

00:43:14.060 --> 00:43:18.210
Now what about confounder selection.

00:43:18.210 --> 00:43:20.100
This is another ongoing problem.

00:43:20.100 --> 00:43:26.970
Again, if there are thousands of confounders,
then picking the right ones may not be as

00:43:26.970 --> 00:43:33.890
simple as just fitting a propensity score
model using variable selection techniques.

00:43:33.890 --> 00:43:40.500
So here we need a different kind of loss function,
because we need to pick confounders that,

00:43:40.500 --> 00:43:43.800
not just meet the best prediction and the
propensity score of the outcome, but that

00:43:43.800 --> 00:43:57.870
also satisfy as much as possible the treatment
ignorability assumption.

00:43:57.870 --> 00:44:01.870
So inevitably, in causal inference, we're
confronted with the problem of unmeasured

00:44:01.870 --> 00:44:03.810
confounding.

00:44:03.810 --> 00:44:11.020
And as I mentioned before, the conundrum of
causal difference is that we never get to

00:44:11.020 --> 00:44:12.800
see the other potential outcome.

00:44:12.800 --> 00:44:19.980
And so it's not possible to test the "no unmeasured
confounding" null hypothesis.

00:44:19.980 --> 00:44:24.170
So there's been a lot of recent interest focused
on sensitivity analysis and quantification

00:44:24.170 --> 00:44:26.720
of this source of uncertainty.

00:44:26.720 --> 00:44:29.260
And I'll just briefly mention two.

00:44:29.260 --> 00:44:33.040
One is a method where we can quantify the
magnitude of potential biases.

00:44:33.040 --> 00:44:40.070
And the other is known as a tipping point
analysis in which we figure out how much unmeasured

00:44:40.070 --> 00:44:46.160
confounding does there have to be for my results
to change.

00:44:46.160 --> 00:44:54.220
So it's often helpful to think about when
we need a sensitivity analysis.

00:44:54.220 --> 00:44:59.040
So here's a simple graphical model that shows
some causal relationships.

00:44:59.040 --> 00:45:01.780
We see that A is treatment.

00:45:01.780 --> 00:45:05.430
And we're interested in the causal relationship
between A and Y.

00:45:05.430 --> 00:45:10.040
We see that X are the confounders, the observed
confounders.

00:45:10.040 --> 00:45:15.550
And they might be related to both A and Y.

00:45:15.550 --> 00:45:24.030
And I'll skip ahead here to show that there
may be unmeasured confounders that are also

00:45:24.030 --> 00:45:26.730
related to both A and Y.

00:45:26.730 --> 00:45:33.070
And we need to adjust for the bias associated
with those unmeasured confounders.

00:45:33.070 --> 00:45:38.910
And as I mentioned before, there's no way
to know whether unmeasured confounding exists.

00:45:38.910 --> 00:45:44.470
So we can flip the question around and ask
a question like, how strong does unmeasured

00:45:44.470 --> 00:45:49.270
confounding have to be in order to change
the results of a significant finding?

00:45:49.270 --> 00:45:55.000
So what I'm going to show here is a simple
version of what's called a tipping point analysis,

00:45:55.000 --> 00:45:56.000
using the HERS data.

00:45:56.000 --> 00:45:58.920
And this is due to Paul Rosenbaum.

00:45:58.920 --> 00:46:02.830
And there's a really terrific review paper
in Statistical Science that I've cited here

00:46:02.830 --> 00:46:06.070
that I recommend reading.

00:46:06.070 --> 00:46:12.040
And it has a lot of very good discussion papers
as well following it.

00:46:12.040 --> 00:46:17.380
So what Paul recommends is, in the case of
matched pairs, to think of there being an

00:46:17.380 --> 00:46:23.090
unmeasured confounder, U, that affects treatment
assignment within the matched pair.

00:46:23.090 --> 00:46:30.410
And if we frame the unmeasured confounder
as a binary variable that has odds ratio,

00:46:30.410 --> 00:46:37.770
gamma, what this tells us is that within matched
pairs, based on X, the odds of receiving treatment

00:46:37.770 --> 00:46:45.660
is gamma times higher among those with U equals
1 for the unmeasured confounder.

00:46:45.660 --> 00:46:53.730
So this yields a sensitivity analysis that
can be represented, as in this table.

00:46:53.730 --> 00:46:56.010
So in the first column, we have gamma.

00:46:56.010 --> 00:46:59.910
In the second column, we have the estimated
causal effect.

00:46:59.910 --> 00:47:03.790
In the third column, there's a p-value associated
with the causal effect.

00:47:03.790 --> 00:47:08.680
And the one in red is the estimated causal
effect.

00:47:08.680 --> 00:47:10.480
This is actually using a ranked test.

00:47:10.480 --> 00:47:14.250
So it's slightly different than what we've
seen in previous slides.

00:47:14.250 --> 00:47:20.370
But the p-value is 0.002, indicating a significant
causal effect of treatment.

00:47:20.370 --> 00:47:30.640
But p-value switches to something greater
than 0.05 when gamma is at 1.33.

00:47:30.640 --> 00:47:35.180
So what that says is that if there's an unmeasured
confounder that changes the odds of receiving

00:47:35.180 --> 00:47:47.270
treatment by 30% or more, then this could
turn our results from significant to non-significant.

00:47:47.270 --> 00:47:52.160
So another kind of approach is what's called
a bias analysis, where we ask ourselves, how

00:47:52.160 --> 00:47:56.550
sensitive are our results to additional bias
from unmeasured confounding?

00:47:56.550 --> 00:48:01.400
And the differences between this and the tipping
point analysis is that the bias analysis

00:48:01.400 --> 00:48:05.930
is measured on the scale of the outcome.

00:48:05.930 --> 00:48:11.570
So others show you an example from a paper
by Steve Cole and others.

00:48:11.570 --> 00:48:16.980
And this is also looking at treatment effects,
looking at the effect of HIV treatment on

00:48:16.980 --> 00:48:19.360
CD4 cell count.

00:48:19.360 --> 00:48:25.060
And what we see in this table is, what I've
circled in the first part of the table is

00:48:25.060 --> 00:48:30.990
an unadjusted effect computed from observed
data, which is 21.5.

00:48:30.990 --> 00:48:34.470
And there's an adjusted effect, which is adjusted
for baseline confounders.

00:48:34.470 --> 00:48:35.990
And the adjusted effect is 71.

00:48:35.990 --> 00:48:39.620
So we see the same sort of phenomenon that
I showed you in the HERS study.

00:48:39.620 --> 00:48:46.970
And the observed confounding bias is on the
order of 49.5, meaning that the treatment

00:48:46.970 --> 00:48:52.400
effect changed by 49 units after controlling
for confounders.

00:48:52.400 --> 00:49:04.660
So the bias analysis, then, looks at the potential
for bias if there was additional unmeasured

00:49:04.660 --> 00:49:05.660
confounding.

00:49:05.660 --> 00:49:09.480
It's on the x-axis we'd see the degree of
bias from unmeasured confounding.

00:49:09.480 --> 00:49:14.510
And on the y-axis what we see is how much
our results would change.

00:49:14.510 --> 00:49:22.560
So if we look at the dashed line and we trace
the x-axis from 0 back to negative 50, we

00:49:22.560 --> 00:49:28.660
would be looking at scenarios where the observed
covariates didn't tell us enough about the

00:49:28.660 --> 00:49:33.430
unmeasured confounding in the case where unmeasured
confounding meant that sicker patients were

00:49:33.430 --> 00:49:35.400
more likely to be treated.

00:49:35.400 --> 00:49:39.280
So this could represent a scenario, for example,
where doctors have access to information that's

00:49:39.280 --> 00:49:41.400
not recorded in the study.

00:49:41.400 --> 00:49:46.740
And it leads them to treat their patients
more preferentially when they appear less

00:49:46.740 --> 00:49:54.200
healthy.

00:49:54.200 --> 00:49:58.360
So I know we're coming up against the end
of the time period.

00:49:58.360 --> 00:50:02.310
So what I would like to do is just go through
these slides.

00:50:02.310 --> 00:50:07.980
And I've put some references in that will
be helpful if you want to start to look into

00:50:07.980 --> 00:50:12.590
some of these issues.

00:50:12.590 --> 00:50:17.120
I'll just mention a few topics that are really
quite active areas of causal inference, at

00:50:17.120 --> 00:50:18.930
least in health research.

00:50:18.930 --> 00:50:20.940
It's not an exhaustive list.

00:50:20.940 --> 00:50:26.750
But these are topics that I think people are
seeing as quite important.

00:50:26.750 --> 00:50:36.890
So mediation analysis is a question that looks
at modeling causal processes over time.

00:50:36.890 --> 00:50:47.060
And this is particularly popular in behavioral
science, where behavioral interventions themselves

00:50:47.060 --> 00:50:53.770
don't typically cause outcomes, but they typically
act by changing intermediate variables.

00:50:53.770 --> 00:50:57.490
So for example, we might have a cognitive
therapy intervention.

00:50:57.490 --> 00:51:03.840
The intervention is not like a drug that acts
physically on an individual, but it works

00:51:03.840 --> 00:51:07.770
through intermediate variables to change the
behavior.

00:51:07.770 --> 00:51:12.660
And mediation analysis is about, number one,
testing hypotheses about mediators, but number

00:51:12.660 --> 00:51:19.230
two, on discovering which variables from a
list are potential mediating variables.

00:51:19.230 --> 00:51:23.690
So I'll skip a couple of these slides.

00:51:23.690 --> 00:51:26.660
Another really important topic is causal inference
for networks.

00:51:26.660 --> 00:51:33.520
And the main problem here is that in the simple
example I showed you, individuals are independent

00:51:33.520 --> 00:51:35.080
of each other.

00:51:35.080 --> 00:51:41.290
But in networks, for example, when we're studying
the efficacy of a vaccine, the outcomes for

00:51:41.290 --> 00:51:44.440
individuals are not at all independent.

00:51:44.440 --> 00:51:48.110
So in the case of vaccines, it's easy to see
why.

00:51:48.110 --> 00:51:53.970
If a person is vaccinated, they're less likely
to transmit the disease to another person,

00:51:53.970 --> 00:51:56.390
whether they're vaccinated or not.

00:51:56.390 --> 00:52:02.390
And these raise very thorny and complicated
issues related to interference and network

00:52:02.390 --> 00:52:03.440
structure.

00:52:03.440 --> 00:52:08.150
So I've just given a couple of references
here to get you started.

00:52:08.150 --> 00:52:13.890
Brain network connectivity is another very
active area of research where researchers

00:52:13.890 --> 00:52:19.230
are trying to understand how signals pass
through different regions of the brain and

00:52:19.230 --> 00:52:23.610
how those signals are processed.

00:52:23.610 --> 00:52:27.510
Precision medicine is an area that demands
a lot of causal thinking.

00:52:27.510 --> 00:52:33.680
It's easy to get confused, in this case, between
causal and predictive inference.

00:52:33.680 --> 00:52:37.200
Because purely empirical approaches have a
lot of pitfalls.

00:52:37.200 --> 00:52:41.690
And I think what's emerging is a consensus
that design trials are likely to generate

00:52:41.690 --> 00:52:49.160
the best primary evidence about precision
medicine and personalized therapies.

00:52:49.160 --> 00:53:00.320
So I've given a couple of references here
to get started.

00:53:00.320 --> 00:53:08.490
So one innovative use of instrumental variables
which I didn't mention much is Mendelian randomization.

00:53:08.490 --> 00:53:13.350
Researchers use genetic mutations or variants
to study causal effects of disease.

00:53:13.350 --> 00:53:20.250
Here's an example where sickle cell trait
is a genetic trait that's related to development

00:53:20.250 --> 00:53:23.020
of malaria.

00:53:23.020 --> 00:53:30.490
And this can be used to study whether malaria
affects growth in children.

00:53:30.490 --> 00:53:35.810
I'll just refer you to this paper to learn
more about it, but this is becoming an emerging

00:53:35.810 --> 00:53:41.880
area of research in public health.

00:53:41.880 --> 00:53:47.940
So in summary, I would just like to say that
causal inference is more than a set of fancy

00:53:47.940 --> 00:53:48.940
techniques.

00:53:48.940 --> 00:53:54.420
And sometimes I get worried that researchers
just think of it as a cornucopia of different

00:53:54.420 --> 00:53:55.420
techniques.

00:53:55.420 --> 00:54:01.700
It's really a systematic way of thinking about
a problem or a question at hand.

00:54:01.700 --> 00:54:07.450
And counter-factual thinking, to my mind at
least, is very important to framing causal

00:54:07.450 --> 00:54:10.530
effects or framing causal questions.

00:54:10.530 --> 00:54:17.170
And with big data, there's a big temptation
to be lured into the trap of discovering causal

00:54:17.170 --> 00:54:20.250
relationships on a purely empirical basis.

00:54:20.250 --> 00:54:23.080
I think of this as data first, science later.

00:54:23.080 --> 00:54:28.860
Where, we might turn loose some fancy analytic
techniques on big data, and then possibly

00:54:28.860 --> 00:54:32.110
retrofit scientific explanations.

00:54:32.110 --> 00:54:36.250
With causal inference, I think that it's really
important to have a careful formulation of

00:54:36.250 --> 00:54:37.440
a model first.

00:54:37.440 --> 00:54:40.080
The science should come first.

00:54:40.080 --> 00:54:45.870
And then the data, whether it's observed data,
found data, or coming from an experiment,

00:54:45.870 --> 00:54:47.660
comes later.

00:54:47.660 --> 00:54:55.630
And the inference process in causal inference
focuses on the model, of course, but then

00:54:55.630 --> 00:55:00.220
what assumptions are testable, what assumptions
are not testable, which is really just as

00:55:00.220 --> 00:55:07.990
important, and properly reflecting uncertainty
from both sampling variation and from untestable

00:55:07.990 --> 00:55:09.670
assumptions.

00:55:09.670 --> 00:55:13.210
So I'll stop there to take questions.

00:55:13.210 --> 00:55:16.470
And I'm sorry for going over by about five
minutes.

00:55:16.470 --> 00:55:23.000
I can, if needed, stay on for a couple minutes
longer.

00:55:23.000 --> 00:55:25.440
Thanks for this great introduction to causal
inference.

00:55:25.440 --> 00:55:29.120
So we have a couple of questions from our
audience.

00:55:29.120 --> 00:55:35.930
So first question is, if we want to infer
causality longitudinal data, can we apply

00:55:35.930 --> 00:55:41.830
the same types of analyses or approach that
you've described in this lecture?

00:55:41.830 --> 00:55:47.190
So obviously, you've touched a little bit
upon this, but could you elaborate further

00:55:47.190 --> 00:55:56.850
on how longitudinal data analysis would be
looked at in terms of causal analysis?

00:55:56.850 --> 00:56:06.250
There's lots of versions of causal questions
that apply to longitudinal data.

00:56:06.250 --> 00:56:13.490
And just thinking about it a little bit in
real time, I'm trying to organize it.

00:56:13.490 --> 00:56:21.960
But I would organize it into settings where
the treatment is stochastic, meaning that

00:56:21.960 --> 00:56:28.150
it can change over time in response to an
individual's evolving health history.

00:56:28.150 --> 00:56:32.380
And in cases where the treatment is static,
meaning that it's assigned at the beginning

00:56:32.380 --> 00:56:35.970
and we look at longitudinal trajectories over
time.

00:56:35.970 --> 00:56:46.550
I think in the latter case, any of the methods
that I described could be used.

00:56:46.550 --> 00:56:53.190
If the treatment is static, meaning that it's
assigned at a fixed point in time, then you

00:56:53.190 --> 00:56:57.453
don't have to model the stochastic nature,
necessarily, of the treatment and its relation

00:56:57.453 --> 00:56:59.700
to outcomes.

00:56:59.700 --> 00:57:07.670
But when the treatment changes over time,
then we're restricted in the types of methods

00:57:07.670 --> 00:57:08.670
that we can use.

00:57:08.670 --> 00:57:13.490
And in particular, matching becomes much more
difficult.

00:57:13.490 --> 00:57:21.410
So the methods that I'm familiar with for
dealing with longitudinal treatments are inverse

00:57:21.410 --> 00:57:26.710
probability weighting and G-computation algorithm.

00:57:26.710 --> 00:57:30.690
Basically, you can just think of it as extrapolation.

00:57:30.690 --> 00:57:37.290
And the idea is that we have to fit two types
of models in order to extract inference.

00:57:37.290 --> 00:57:42.950
One is, we have to fit a model that tells
us how the treatment is evolving in response

00:57:42.950 --> 00:57:45.880
to the subject's evolving history.

00:57:45.880 --> 00:57:53.180
And the other is, how the outcome is evolving
in response to treatment and prior history.

00:57:53.180 --> 00:58:04.140
The inverse weighting technique and the extrapolation
or G-estimation techniques can be used in

00:58:04.140 --> 00:58:06.450
those settings.

00:58:06.450 --> 00:58:11.240
Setting aside techniques for a minute, something
that I think is really important is that in

00:58:11.240 --> 00:58:15.950
longitudinal settings where the treatment
can vary over time, this is where graphical

00:58:15.950 --> 00:58:20.630
models play a really important role.

00:58:20.630 --> 00:58:26.000
And I regret that I didn't have a lot of time
to go into those, but the recommendation I

00:58:26.000 --> 00:58:33.180
would make is to look into the literature
on graphical models.

00:58:33.180 --> 00:58:35.480
Because they give us a starting point.

00:58:35.480 --> 00:58:39.410
And they tell us like what assumptions are
needed before we start fitting models and

00:58:39.410 --> 00:58:41.280
using analytic techniques.

00:58:41.280 --> 00:58:47.350
Judea Pearl has a really nice review that
I could pass along if there's a way.

00:58:47.350 --> 00:58:51.750
I think it's in Health Services and Outcomes
Research journal.

00:58:51.750 --> 00:58:57.340
It's called "Causality for Health Sciences,"
I believe.

00:58:57.340 --> 00:59:06.450
And Thomas Richardson and Jamie Robins have
worked extensively in this area developing

00:59:06.450 --> 00:59:11.710
graphical models for complex longitudinal
processes.

00:59:11.710 --> 00:59:17.240
Another question that we have, we're actually
getting quite a few from the audience.

00:59:17.240 --> 00:59:22.170
So using your first example, is it fair to
say that causal inference is useful in clinical

00:59:22.170 --> 00:59:27.660
trials to potentially detect potential biases
in the randomization process.

00:59:27.660 --> 00:59:33.550
Because randomization may not be fully random
or as expected.

00:59:33.550 --> 00:59:41.330
Well, let's see, I'm not a clinical trials
expert.

00:59:41.330 --> 00:59:44.570
So I don't want to go too far out on a limb
here.

00:59:44.570 --> 00:59:53.570
But I think that most randomized trials that
I've been involved in, even if there are small

00:59:53.570 --> 00:59:58.480
discrepancies in the randomization, they don't
turn into significant problems.

00:59:58.480 --> 01:00:06.920
Typically, if there's problems with imbalance,
it's more a function of the trial execution.

01:00:06.920 --> 01:00:13.070
And I guess if there are really severe problems
in the differences between groups at baseline,

01:00:13.070 --> 01:00:15.510
the randomized trial
would almost have to be treated as an observational

01:00:15.510 --> 01:00:16.640
study.

01:00:16.640 --> 01:00:23.220
So if I'm understanding the question right,
causal evidence methods could be used to correct

01:00:23.220 --> 01:00:28.970
significant discrepancies between the groups.

